6. The case of defendant No 1 is that dargah of Hazrat Shah Jalal (R) at Sylhet was enrolled as Public Waqf in 1951 with Mr. AbdusJafar Abdullah as Mutawalli as per provision of Bengal Waqf Act, 1934 which was repealed and re-enacted by East Pakistan Waqf Ordinance I of 1962 and this Ordinance came into force on 19.06.1962 giving Administrator of Waqf wide power of supervision and control over the waqf estate. Soon after the ordinance came into force, Mr. S.N.H. Rizvi, the then Administrator, passed order to function as sole Mutawalli and was to be responsible for all accounts, income and expenditure. He also passed order gave direction as to how the 'Nazars' are to be collected as there was a large number of 'khadims' who were collecting some 'Nazars' from the visitors. With the aforesaid order taking away the right of the Khadims to collect 'Nazar,' the 'Mutawalli and the 'khadims' were given ration not exceeding 10% of the total income of the Waqf. Some of the 'Khadims' considered themselves aggrieved by this order taking away their rights to collect Nazars form the visitors made petition to the District Judge challenging the legality of the Administrator's order and contending that the order adversely affected their ' Bcridari right.' Learned Judge by his order dated 10.07.1962, set aside the order of the Administrator of Waqf, against which. Administrator of Waqfs preferred appeal before the High Court Division being First Miscellaneous Appeal No. 03 of 1962; their Lordships by their judgment dated 08.08.1967 observed that order of the Administrator of Waqf was not binding on the 'khadims' as no notice was served to them and the Administration could however proceed to pass necessary order in the matter after giving notice to the 'khadims.' Thereafter, 'Khadims' were given notice by the Administrator of Waqf. The Khadims described their 'Baridari right in the petition of appeal before the learned District Judge. 

 25. In Title Suit No. 132 of 1902 defendant No. 3 contested on the ground that 'female cannot be a Khadims, and there is no custom or law sanctioning it. Such a practice is harmful. It never existed. and h is opposed to the doctrines of Mohamedan law and usage. If a Khadim dies, leaving sons and daughters or wife. as his heirs, no change takes place in the days of his turn. It has been further contended on behalf of defendant No. 3 that the real facts of the case are that Surat Bibi having thus died, without leaving any heir recognized by Mohamedan law. and her Khadimi Baridair right having devolved upon defendant No. 3 as the Sarkum and manually of the Dargah.' We find that both the courts below decreed the suit but on appeal it was reversed as aforesaid. This matter refers to Baridari right of Khadims of the Dargah of Hazrat Shah Sylhet claimed for consideration upon objection filed under section 50 of the East Bengal Waqf Ordinance. 1962. Administrator of Waqf East Pakistan on 10.07.1962 passed order as hereunder: 

 Explanation-when more than fifty per cent of the net available income of a waqf is exclusively applied for religious and charitable purposes, such a waqf shall be deemed to be a public waqf within the meaning of clause (e) of sub-section (I) of section 85 of the East Bengal Non-Agricultural Tenancy Act, 1949 (EB Act XXIII of 1949)and shall be deemed to be a trust for public purposes of a charitable or religious nature within the meaning of section 92 of the Code of Civil procedure, 1908 (Act V of 1908) and section 14 of the Religious Endowments Act, 1863 (Act XX of 1863);
